{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"a3182fef-eaa3-4280-97fa-6356a420cc0a","step_index":32,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","duration_seconds":0.071243,"tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"grep -rn \"worklore.dev/s\" notes/"},"output":"\u003ctruncated 1 lines\u003e\nnotes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md:45:2. **C2 · A tool refusal comes back as a result, not a protocol error.** Call a read-only tool with an id that does not exist, and separately with a required argument missing. PASS: HTTP 200 with a `result` that has `isError: true` and a readable message. FAIL: a JSON-RPC `error` (typically `-32602`) — a client SDK throws on that, so the model never reads \"not found\" and cannot correct itself; the 2025-11-25 spec revision moved input validation into tool results ([SEP-1303](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/community/seps/1303-input-validation-errors-as-tool-execution-errors)). An UNKNOWN tool name may stay a JSON-RPC error. Fix: [My live MCP test asserted the bug: a real client threw where my test passed](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-10-02-my-live-mcp-test-asserted-the-bug-a-real-client-threw-where-).\r\nnotes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md:49:4. **C4 · The published outputSchema is honoured.** Keep the schemas from the `tools/list` response — not from the source code. For every read-only tool that declares an `outputSchema`, make one call with valid arguments. PASS: no `isError`, `structuredContent` is present, and it validates against that schema (types, `required` fields, nested objects and arrays). FAIL: `isError` on a valid call, a missing `structuredContent`, or a mismatch — clients that validate results will reject them. An error result proves nothing about the schema, so it is a FAIL here, not a pass. Fix: [My tests passed because they agreed with me](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-28-my-tests-passed-because-they-agreed-with-me).\r\nnotes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md:53:6. **C6 · The version moves when the surface moves.** First, check whether the server publishes a fingerprint: read its official MCP Registry entry (`GET https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/v0.1/servers/\u003cname\u003e/versions/\u003cversion\u003e`) and look under `_meta` → `io.modelcontextprotocol.registry/publisher-provided` for a block with `\"form\": \"mcp-surface/1\"`. That is a shared format ([the spec](https://github.com/SidneyBissoli/mcp-br-commons/blob/main/packages/mcp-surface/SPEC.md)); if it is there, run its independent checker, [`verify.mjs`](https://github.com/SidneyBissoli/mcp-br-commons/blob/main/packages/mcp-surface/exemplos/verify.mjs) (`node verify.mjs \u003cname\u003e \u003cversion\u003e`, Node only, no dependencies), and use its verdict. If there is no such block, compute the fingerprint yourself by the same spec (the `initialize` result without `serverInfo.version`, plus `tools/list`, `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list` and `prompts/list`, normalised and hashed as its §2–§3 say) and report it with `serverInfo.version`; compare the Registry's latest version with `serverInfo.version`. If you have the source, look for a committed surface lock (a file holding the version and this hash, checked by a test). Also look at what the lists contain: a list that changes by itself (stories, records, daily files) cannot be in a version promise, because its hash changes every day while nothing breaks; such items belong behind a resource template or a tool. PASS: `verify.mjs` passes, or the Registry version equals the live version and a lock exists. FAIL: `verify.mjs` reports a mismatch, or the Registry lists a different version than the live server reports — the listing describes a server that no longer exists. WARN (count as FAIL for \"has the problem\"): no lock, so nothing stops the next drift; or the fingerprint covers a list that changes by itself. NOT CHECKED: no registry entry and no source. Fix: [My MCP listing said read-only. My server could write.](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-30-my-mcp-listing-said-read-only-my-server-could-write), and for republishing the entry, [Getting into the MCP Registry](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-23-getting-into-the-mcp-registry-server-json-a-read-org-token-a).\r\nnotes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md:55:7. **C7 · Calls are logged.** From outside this cannot be seen, so: if you have the source, find the single place every JSON-RPC call passes through and check that it writes one structured line per call (method, tool name, ok/error, latency, who). If you have log access, look for the calls you just made. PASS: one line per call, errors included. FAIL: no per-call log, or only an HTTP access log (every line is `POST /mcp 200`, which tells you nothing). NOT CHECKED: no source and no logs. Fix: [Your MCP server is one URL — getting per-tool, per-user analytics anyway](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-23-your-mcp-server-is-one-url-getting-per-tool-per-user-analyti).\r\nnotes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md:57:8. **C8 · Tool count and names.** From `tools/list`: report the count, and check every name against `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$`. PASS: all match. FAIL: any name with a dot, space or other character, or longer than 64 — OpenAI's function-name rule rejects it, and Anthropic's has the same shape, so whole clients cannot call that tool, whatever a directory scorecard says about dotted names. Names are hard to change once a registry has scanned you. Fix: [An MCP server for the price of one more endpoint](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-25-an-mcp-server-for-the-price-of-one-more-endpoint) (step 1), and [Submitting an MCP server to the directories](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-23-submitting-an-mcp-server-to-the-directories-one-cli-one-gith) on the scorecard trap.\r\nnotes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md:59:9. **C9 · Version negotiation is honest.** Send `initialize` with `\"protocolVersion\": \"banana\"`. PASS: the server answers with a version it really implements (not \"banana\"), or a clear error. FAIL: it echoes \"banana\" — it would claim any future revision too. Fix: [An MCP server for the price of one more endpoint](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-25-an-mcp-server-for-the-price-of-one-more-endpoint) (Verify section).\r\nnotes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md:61:10. **C10 · The outputSchema can reject a wrong answer.** Use the `tools/list` response from C1 and call nothing. For every tool that declares an `outputSchema`, build a corrupted result with the same structure: include the `required` properties (all properties if none are required), follow `$ref` into `$defs`, take the first branch of any `anyOf`/`oneOf`/`allOf`, and set every string to `\"mcp-drill-corruption\"`, every integer to `-999999999`, every number to `-1.5e308`, every boolean to `false`. Validate it against the schema with a real JSON Schema validator (Python `jsonschema`, JS `ajv`), or run [mcp-drill](https://github.com/TimurRakhmatullin86/mcp-drill) (`mcp-drill scan --url \u003cserver\u003e`). PASS: every schema rejects the corrupted result because of a value constraint (`enum`, `const`, `pattern`, a bound) or a required field. FAIL: any schema accepts it — the contract cannot tell a wrong answer from a right one, so a client that validates learns nothing. A lone `type` error at a `$ref` means the builder did not follow the reference: count that tool as FAIL. NOT CHECKED: no tool declares an `outputSchema` (it is optional); say so in the report. Fix: [My MCP server's output schemas accepted wrong answers](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-10-15-my-mcp-server-s-output-schemas-accepted-wrong-answers-7-of-8).\r\n"}}}
